Um, so I think that was, that was super helpful in the short term, like in the, in the week, the week immediately after where it was most painful, just getting lost in my work and being around people and not being by myself and not feeling, you know, sorry for myself.

00:24:37.000 --> 00:24:54.000
Um, and just trying not, trying not to think about it, uh, helped me at least, uh, you know, he may already be past that stage now in, in, um, you know, where he's at with his recovery. Uh, but I found that super helpful for me.

00:24:54.000 --> 00:25:14.000
Uh, the next part is more of a mental game as well as a self-confidence game where, you know, when you're dealing with rejection, whether it's a, you know, a relationship or a client doesn't need you, doesn't want to work with you anymore or, you know, whatever it is.

00:25:14.000 --> 00:25:33.000
Um, it's understanding that it is probably for everybody's best, right? Like if somebody really doesn't want to be with you, then why would you want to be with them too? Uh, there's, there's somebody better out there for you.

00:25:33.000 --> 00:25:46.000
Not that there's, not that there's plenty of fish in the sea and there's, you know, like tons of people, but there's, there's one person out there that's, that's better for you and more aligned with you, um, than what you had.

00:25:46.000 --> 00:26:00.000
And same thing with clients. You know, there's, if the client doesn't want to work with you, there is better clients out there for you. You don't have to go be somebody that you're not to try to appease one client.

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:16.000
And no matter how much you try, it just doesn't seem to be working out. The best thing to do is probably leave that client and go do something else. And it sucks when you're the one kind of giving more to the relationship and you really want it to work out and it's not.

00:26:16.000 --> 00:26:38.000
Um, so I, it's, it's, uh, it's easy to say, but hard to do. It's hard to actually feel it. Uh, but you know, with time, at least I've found that the more you tell yourself that and the more you start to believe it because it is actually true and is important part of, uh, of healing.

00:26:38.000 --> 00:26:58.000
Um, the second and maybe deeper issue is not putting so much of your self worth, uh, in somebody else's hands. And that takes a ton of practice and it's not something that, you know, we're really taught in schools.

00:26:58.000 --> 00:27:12.000
But we're doing a video and then espresso coming up soon with, uh, Tyrese Gibson, the guy from the Fast and the Furious. And he has a great little speech that he goes off on.

00:27:12.000 --> 00:27:40.000
On, uh, not like I know I'm great. You know, whether you stop thinking I'm great, I still know that I'm great. Um, maybe TD Jakes? No, it's not TD Jakes. It's Quincy Jones who said something similar where it's, uh, you know, not one ounce of my self worth is tied up in how you think of me.

00:27:40.000 --> 00:27:53.000
Uh, and I think, especially when it comes to rejection and relationships, we care. If you want to have a successful relationship, at least what I found is you need to love yourself first.

00:27:53.000 --> 00:28:16.000
You need to be happy with who you are first and not need that other person to give you the validation that you are good, that you are acceptable. Uh, and you shouldn't be able to, if you can bring that to a relationship, it makes it much stronger.

00:28:16.000 --> 00:28:29.000
Uh, I feel like I'm happy with my life and I want you in it, not, I need you here to be happy because it also puts an unfair amount of pressure on your partner.

00:28:29.000 --> 00:28:41.000
Uh, where if you can both enter a relationship with that mindset mentality, um, you're much more likely to, uh, to have success, at least in my humble opinion.

